            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-000000000007",
            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-000000000008",
            "visible": true,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-000000000009",
            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-00000000000a",
            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-00000000000b",
            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-00000000000c",
            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-00000000000d",
            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          }
        ],
        "content": {
          "attachments": {
            "fig-before-after.png": {
              "image/png": ""
            }
          },
          "contentMode": "markdown",
          "image": "",
          "imageCaption": "",
          "markdown": "**Before anything was counted, this is what happened to the valley.**\n\n![The Bhote Koshi at Timure, before and after](attachment:fig-before-after.png)\n\nThe river was a thread through forest. It is now a pale band several times\nwider, scoured to bare rock and gravel from wall to wall. Everything the rest of\nthis map counts happened inside that band.\n\nNo single satellite pass shows this. Every one after the flood is 70 to 90%\ncloud. Both sides here are built by taking, for each pixel, the middle of\nwhatever cloud-free looks exist across several passes. The pale grey is ground\nthat never got a clear look in the window, and it is left empty rather than\nguessed at.\n\nWhich is the same problem the rest of the map has, in picture form: **what you\ncan see depends on when the sky happened to be open.**\n\nSharper pictures of this exist. Copernicus graded the damage on Pléiades Neo and\nWorldView-3 at **0.3 m**, UNOSAT used CARTOSAT-3, HOT used SkySat. All of it is\ncommercial imagery, published inside their map products and reserved otherwise,\nso none of it can travel in a dataset like this one. What you are looking at is\nthe best picture that can be **given away** rather than the best picture taken.\n",
          "paneAlignment": "start",
          "panelWidth": "34%",
          "title": "What the river looks like now"
        }
      }
    },
    "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8001-000000000004": {
      "name": "The shape the first map guessed, and the shape that happened",
      "type": "StorySegmentLayer",
      "visible": true,
      "parameters": {
        "zoom": 11.0,
        "extent": [
          9445573.6682308,
          3225878.46695133,
